, "insertedAt": "2026-06-17T10:42:05.984Z", "publishedAt": "2026-06-17T06:19:00.300Z", "media": [{"url": "https://wechat2rss.bestblogs.dev/img-proxy/?k=76ce1083&u=https%3A%2F%2Fmmbiz.qpic.cn%2Fsz_mmbiz_jpg%2FDthwRd8vvp0W9vVbCCibHicu8v9ArfWDHtM7RXqI3d49TCDFvXr3QWbjcibKX5PSWOSAXdcyBFcKicvsk06spuZdaTbmPHU7Rk6ySb0ZEHVmiaDE%2F0%3Fwx_fmt%3Djpeg", "type": "photo", "width": 903, "height": 384}], "categories": null, "attachments": null, "extra": {}, "language": null, "summary": "Zhejiang University economist Yuan Zhe evaluates the \"2026 China E-commerce AI Application White Paper,\" arguing AI's significance lies in democratizing business capabilities rather than just efficiency gains. His research based on Alibaba platform data shows AI tools have shifted from \"technology access\" to \"business integration,\" enabling small merchants to access capabilities previously affordable only by large enterprises. Small businesses with under 20,000 yuan sales achieved 75.8% growth using generative AI tools—nearly four times large companies' growth. Yuan frames this as \"business capability equalization,\" though notes new digital divides are emerging in AI usage depth and organizational absorption capacity."}, "settings": {}}